00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:06.320
by making it that much easier to type things. next&nbsp;
up is pep 612 with parameter specifications. param&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:12.640
specs are meant to help users type hint higher&nbsp;
order functions. similar to a type variable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.680
a param spec will match whatever parameters of&nbsp;
a function are given. this would allow you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.360
say take in any function and return a function&nbsp;
that has the same signature. here is an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.880
of using a param spec from the pep. we create&nbsp;
a param spec just like we do a type variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.720
we're going to write this function add logging.&nbsp;
add logging takes in a callable and returns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.000
another callable with the same parameters except&nbsp;
the return type has been changed to an awaitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.680
of R. that means from this function we return&nbsp;
another function with the same parameters as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.120
original but now the return type is an awaitable&nbsp;
of the original because here we're using an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.880
await keyword and an async function. notice&nbsp;
here that we can now properly type hint star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:04.080
args and star star kwargs to be p dot args and&nbsp;
p dot kwargs. this way we can refer specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.640
to the args and kwargs of the original&nbsp;
function's parameters which were captured by P.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.280
this means that whatever parameters the passed in&nbsp;
callable has you can refer to its args and kwargs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:21.760
by accessing p dot args and p dot kwargs. here&nbsp;
are some more examples of signatures of functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.680
that either add, remove, or change parameters.&nbsp;
this function add takes in a callable with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:33.200
parameters that returns an int and it returns&nbsp;
a new callable that has a new additional string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:39.120
parameter along with all the other old parameters.&nbsp;
additionally it now returns a bool. here we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.160
using the newly introduced typing dot concatenate&nbsp;
which can only be used in this context as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:50.320
first argument in a callable. concatenate str p&nbsp;
means that the parameters to this new function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.960
should first be a string followed by all the&nbsp;
parameters that were passed into the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.320
function. we can use concatenate and param spec&nbsp;
in the input of a function instead of the output&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:05.440
to remove parameters instead of add them. here&nbsp;
we're taking in a function which takes an int&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.720
and some other stuff and returns a function which&nbsp;
just takes the other stuff, no more int. next on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:17.680
the list is pep 613 with explicit type aliases.&nbsp;
if you have a recursive data structure or a data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.840
structure where you refer to the type of the&nbsp;
data structure in one of the type hints in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.800
the data structure, you can't use the actual&nbsp;
type of the data structure when you do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.640
this is because within the definition of the data&nbsp;
structure, the data structure is not yet defined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:38.560
so you can't use it. for this reason python allows&nbsp;
you to use strings that name types instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:45.520
the actual types themselves. that part's not new.&nbsp;
what is new is that now you can explicitly mark a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:51.360
string as being a type alias, meaning it shouldn't&nbsp;
be used as just another string object, it should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:57.360
only be used as a type hint. this is mainly to&nbsp;
help type checkers from getting confused between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.240
global string declarations that might look like&nbsp;
types and actual things that are supposed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.280
types. you would use a type alias in much the&nbsp;
same kind of situation that you would use a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:13.200
forward declaration in c or c plus plus. you're&nbsp;
essentially telling the type checker i'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:20.240
to define a type with this name in the future.&nbsp;
next up, pep 647 with user defined type guards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:27.760
static type checking is fundamentally limited in a&nbsp;
dynamic language. there are some situations where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.120
a type checker can infer more information on a&nbsp;
type based off of what's around the statements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:38.320
in this case val is an optional string&nbsp;
which means it's either a string or none.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:45.600
we check to see if val is not none. if val isn't&nbsp;
none then within this if block we can assume that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:51.440
it's a string. type checkers today already do&nbsp;
this. similarly in the else block we know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.320
it must be none. there are already a few cases&nbsp;
where type checkers can narrow down the type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:02.640
based off of code execution. for instance you can&nbsp;
narrow down a union using an is instance check,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:08.240
or you can narrow down a literal by using an&nbsp;
equality check. but in some cases the type checker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.440
just can't do it. in this case we go through a&nbsp;
list of objects and check to see if they're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:19.200
instances of strings. if they're all instances&nbsp;
of strings, then this is a list of strings and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:24.400
i should be able to join that list. but type&nbsp;
checkers aren't smart enough to be able to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:31.120
this out. enter user defined type guards. you take&nbsp;
as an argument some object with some type hint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.600
the return type of the function is labeled as type&nbsp;
guard of something. what this actually means is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:43.440
that this function returns a bool that determines&nbsp;
whether or not the input is of the given type.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.640
so if this function returns true then the type&nbsp;
checker should assume that val is actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:57.200
list of str not a list of object. if the function&nbsp;
returns false then no more information is gathered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.160
about the type. if your function takes more&nbsp;
than one parameter, the type guard only applies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.520
to the first argument, it says nothing about the&nbsp;
second or any other arguments. if your function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:12.160
is actually a method or class method though,&nbsp;
then it applies to the second argument, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:18.160
one after self or class. finally there were some&nbsp;
small changes to how the typing literal works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.120
literal no longer cares about duplicate&nbsp;
arguments or the order of its arguments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.840
but it does now care about the type of its&nbsp;
arguments and whether or not its arguments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:33.680
